#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078213 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a2c6bf6-41f2-5f8d-8799-5664ba941dc8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Over an Interval of Length Zero"}] :question/id #uuid "c02d2000-3fef-59e4-9127-84500ef83eb8" :question/math-academy-id "e-418" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_\\pi^\\pi \\sqrt{\\sin x}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the lower and upper limits are the same. So, the integral evaluates to zero:\n\n$$\n\\int_\\pi^\\pi \\sqrt{\\sin x}\\,\\textrm d x = 0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078214 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff2f1a9d-2b0e-5a18-98d5-4f49f6f1527b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Given Another Integral With Reversed Limits of Integration"}] :question/id #uuid "c7a90d29-e8b9-5a06-94f7-776eb254cc52" :question/math-academy-id "e-419" :question/problem "Given that ${\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{6} \\, f(x) \\; \\textrm d x = -54 },$ what is the value of\n${\\displaystyle \\int_{6}^{0} \\bigg( \\dfrac 1 3 f(x) + 1 \\bigg) \\; \\textrm d x }?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{6}^{0}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=54\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{6}^{0}(\\frac{1}{3}f(x)+1)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{6}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{6}^{0}1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 54+x\\,{|}_{6}^{0} \\\\  & =18+(0-6) \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078218 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9693b76e-ba77-5314-bfbf-508b25bb1072" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Given Integrals Over Adjacent Intervals"}] :question/id #uuid "a85f51c5-601b-52ec-82dd-0b611842d3d1" :question/math-academy-id "e-420" :question/problem "If $\\displaystyle \\int_7^{10}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = 18$ and $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{7}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = -6$ then what is $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{10} \\big( 2f(x)+1 \\big) \\,\\textrm d x?$" :question/worked-solution "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{7}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x + \\displaystyle \\int_7^{10}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = \\displaystyle \\int_0^{10}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information into the above gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6+18={\\int }_{0}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 12={\\int }_{0}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the desired integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{10}(2f(x)+1)\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{10}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2(12)+x\\,{|}_{0}^{10} \\\\  & =24+(10-0) \\\\  & =34.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078227 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d752aac-6187-595d-8818-b0179fd7380e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Using Multiple Properties of Definite Integrals"}] :question/id #uuid "3d925b89-dc4d-59e0-bf00-3a3ca90b5169" :question/math-academy-id "e-421" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a  function such that ${\\displaystyle \\int_4^1 2 \\, f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = -1}$ \nand ${\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{-1} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 2}.$  What is the value of ${\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^4 f(x) \\, \\textrm dx}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify the first given integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{1}2\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ 2{\\int }_{4}^{1}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ -2{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second integral can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2 \\\\ -{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, from the adjacent intervals rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287174 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a2c6bf6-41f2-5f8d-8799-5664ba941dc8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Over an Interval of Length Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be7db794-612b-5880-8442-05b9cbe68aab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287176} {:answer/id #uuid "0ec2f26d-1d9d-5499-a6b7-f7ce0454858c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287177} {:answer/id #uuid "4a675bb6-86b1-57a2-b204-c11b82ed4ceb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287178} {:answer/id #uuid "140cdff0-9393-536c-a099-b1d21dd3498b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287179} {:answer/id #uuid "d3b97efb-9a57-5f06-8a15-4af798f97a65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287180}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3b97efb-9a57-5f06-8a15-4af798f97a65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287180} :answer-field/id #uuid "0856f487-a3c2-5792-890b-c3a148c879db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287175}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "11d4d7cf-8fc6-50cc-adb4-6da8f5e57392" :question/math-academy-id "q-114006" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}\\operatorname{cot}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the lower and upper limits are the same. So, the integral evaluates to zero:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}\\operatorname{cot}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287181 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a2c6bf6-41f2-5f8d-8799-5664ba941dc8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Over an Interval of Length Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21491da6-9020-5f0f-a521-24f4e716c99b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287183} {:answer/id #uuid "c193b78c-dc3e-5d10-a29e-2955460d22e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287184} {:answer/id #uuid "8aa107ea-e1b1-5815-82f8-b5a27461623f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287185} {:answer/id #uuid "d113e667-779a-5c10-8674-1aab1b1f572c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287186} {:answer/id #uuid "783649d3-8dbb-5490-97bc-46baa572a583" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "783649d3-8dbb-5490-97bc-46baa572a583" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287187} :answer-field/id #uuid "083c0c36-646e-5368-bba7-7e975e4475f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287182}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "45f26e1c-4606-5ed4-8dd3-eacd6e1c1eee" :question/math-academy-id "q-114007" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{3}^{3}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4x+1}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the lower and upper limits are the same. So, the integral evaluates to zero:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{3}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287188 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a2c6bf6-41f2-5f8d-8799-5664ba941dc8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Over an Interval of Length Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "979f4478-fd2a-5cb3-bce4-df32b0447852" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287190} {:answer/id #uuid "1bdaa589-a2f5-5c00-80e3-20c6a500bb33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287191} {:answer/id #uuid "9f44682d-3b09-580b-ac7f-2e642fbda5dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287192} {:answer/id #uuid "5a8cf248-c947-5987-84a2-86c3becfc679" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287193} {:answer/id #uuid "43bb0a94-6cf8-554a-9ceb-7bf406f39e06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f44682d-3b09-580b-ac7f-2e642fbda5dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287192} :answer-field/id #uuid "10cf95ca-25fc-5901-b95e-f8385f6e9dca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cedc774f-90a1-5b65-8fac-e1951a216be1" :question/math-academy-id "q-114008" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{-1}^{-1}\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the lower and upper limits are the same. So, the integral evaluates to zero:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{-1}\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287195 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff2f1a9d-2b0e-5a18-98d5-4f49f6f1527b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Given Another Integral With Reversed Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "896ccfe9-e0d2-5cb2-a7d8-4c9f69f32be5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287197} {:answer/id #uuid "6f5cb3ea-acaf-5168-ac86-d1e3f33f0640" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287198} {:answer/id #uuid "f431b41c-c2e5-53c1-8627-e3ee92b62559" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287199} {:answer/id #uuid "86983714-6606-55d4-a944-11515976254b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287200} {:answer/id #uuid "078d1099-9af1-5149-ab4f-dcfcfbbf8132" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "896ccfe9-e0d2-5cb2-a7d8-4c9f69f32be5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287197} :answer-field/id #uuid "314d6cc8-3b4e-5237-a59c-14eb4aa364c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287196}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "711122a5-c3d8-5026-8aad-2670f6567d01" :question/math-academy-id "q-114015" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-1,$ calculate\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}(f(x)-5)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(f(x)-5)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x-5{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1-5\\cdot x\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =1-5(2-0) \\\\  & =1-5\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287202 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff2f1a9d-2b0e-5a18-98d5-4f49f6f1527b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Given Another Integral With Reversed Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "232998f1-e760-5ec2-8720-c33cd54e8896" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287204} {:answer/id #uuid "9ba58110-7a69-54fb-89f9-c312fd812114" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287205} {:answer/id #uuid "a119a217-9982-5f31-a9cf-8bc31222630f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287206} {:answer/id #uuid "67b61998-b20a-51e9-abfd-592ab59c9363" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287207} {:answer/id #uuid "d9b85d4d-4ebf-5914-af45-c1628a1f0799" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287208}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a119a217-9982-5f31-a9cf-8bc31222630f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287206} :answer-field/id #uuid "4542caf3-f7b1-5790-8f03-00a198fdb357" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c1c49f11-39cf-55bf-be78-e57de3366735" :question/math-academy-id "q-114017" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=4,$ calculate\n${\\int }_{2}^{4}(7-5f(x))\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}(7-5f(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =7{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\text{d}x-5{\\int }_{2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =7\\cdot x\\,{|}_{2}^{4}-5\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =7(4-2)+20 \\\\  & =7\\cdot 2+20 \\\\  & =34.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287209 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9693b76e-ba77-5314-bfbf-508b25bb1072" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Given Integrals Over Adjacent Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef942b09-8c13-51a3-8ad0-c6f691e4a472" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287211} {:answer/id #uuid "4d7b50e6-5e79-547f-a4d0-3523fadcd174" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287212} {:answer/id #uuid "73a99681-4e73-57fa-9774-b62220810b0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287213} {:answer/id #uuid "6c60c08f-c067-5560-9388-dcc9cd508b05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287214} {:answer/id #uuid "784b28ce-6ef1-5c7e-98aa-25f80beca79e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef942b09-8c13-51a3-8ad0-c6f691e4a472" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287211} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2bc329b-00e9-5c97-812b-73d35b80342e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287210}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2b9f164-4690-5b86-8b79-bf8259387b31" :question/math-academy-id "q-114133" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-2$ and ${\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=3,$ then what is ${\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)+2)\\,\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information into the above gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2+3 & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 1 & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the desired integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)+2)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+2{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+2\\cdot x\\,{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =1+2(3-0) \\\\  & =1+6 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287216 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9693b76e-ba77-5314-bfbf-508b25bb1072" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Given Integrals Over Adjacent Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6315a33-9071-54e2-b9ac-3cb3de8f1320" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287218} {:answer/id #uuid "13fc8e14-533e-556f-8131-fe3dc0d7a59a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287219} {:answer/id #uuid "a7cc3f93-b2e2-55c8-b26f-5f57dbe9c281" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287220} {:answer/id #uuid "353ece7b-f1f6-5933-84f3-a3fcd72ef334" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287221} {:answer/id #uuid "1e342ce5-220b-5b7c-b90f-0a4bad0cedba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6315a33-9071-54e2-b9ac-3cb3de8f1320" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287218} :answer-field/id #uuid "a693a0d1-75c4-5773-a91c-3015b71b45d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287217}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4532d7dd-efba-5785-a721-165bfae74c8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-114135" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=8$ and ${\\int }_{4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=4,$ then what is ${\\int }_{3}^{6}(5-2f(x))\\,\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information into the above gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8+4 & ={\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 12 & ={\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the desired integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{6}(5-2f(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =5{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\text{d}x-2{\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\cdot x\\,{|}_{3}^{6}-2\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =5(6-3)-24 \\\\  & =5(3)-24 \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287223 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d752aac-6187-595d-8818-b0179fd7380e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Using Multiple Properties of Definite Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dad3368-3b1f-5f30-adb6-5b54b1058bda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287225} {:answer/id #uuid "d454aabc-56ca-5c64-970a-a6847e824c37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287226} {:answer/id #uuid "0d1c046d-29e7-576c-bd63-b007a849dacb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287227} {:answer/id #uuid "b5880e00-93d8-50ca-b06b-d51e42517cef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287228} {:answer/id #uuid "2b23a4e3-3cfe-5e99-bd3a-33291a12abb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d1c046d-29e7-576c-bd63-b007a849dacb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287227} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c48bd3e-22c1-5da5-929b-92235c89e5ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287224}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "700eca56-7546-5c46-a5c9-5b46500fdf20" :question/math-academy-id "q-114143" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a function such that ${\\int }_{8}^{5}3f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-2$ and ${\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-3.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{3}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify one of the given integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{8}^{5}3f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-2 \\\\ 3{\\int }_{8}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-2 \\\\ -3{\\int }_{5}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-2 \\\\ {\\int }_{5}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, from the adjacent intervals rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{5}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287230 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9693b76e-ba77-5314-bfbf-508b25bb1072" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Given Integrals Over Adjacent Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a15426d-b21c-5d7c-a006-f1a1b330dc5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a15426d-b21c-5d7c-a006-f1a1b330dc5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287232} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa0f719c-7b6b-5a4d-903c-6743fff769ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287231}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca667e43-98a3-51cf-8ee3-438b59a6ddfc" :question/math-academy-id "q-316099" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-3$ and ${\\int }_{4}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=8,$ \nthen what is ${\\int }_{1}^{7}(2-3f(x))\\,\\text{d}x?$\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{7}(2-3f(x))\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{4}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,=\\,{\\int }_{1}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information gives\n\n$$\n(-3)+8\\,=\\,{\\int }_{1}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,⇒\\,{\\int }_{1}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=5.\n$$\n\nFinally, compute the desired integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{7}(2-3f(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{1}^{7}1\\,\\text{d}x\\,-\\,3{\\int }_{1}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot x{|}_{1}^{7}\\,-\\,3(5) \\\\  & =2(7-1)-15 \\\\  & =12-15 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287233 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a2c6bf6-41f2-5f8d-8799-5664ba941dc8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Over an Interval of Length Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "700afe9e-a82c-5c52-a2c0-c0404193b225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287235} {:answer/id #uuid "3bf0f6ed-1001-5023-9e97-cfdaa68bf963" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3bf0f6ed-1001-5023-9e97-cfdaa68bf963" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287236} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef64d03d-2811-5a9f-b88e-a5cc8f212ec7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287234}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a7768955-20c9-56b9-81ca-a8ca0966c93c" :question/math-academy-id "q-345627" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{5}^{5}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the lower and upper limits are the same. So, the integral evaluates to zero:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{5}^{5}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}x=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287237 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a2c6bf6-41f2-5f8d-8799-5664ba941dc8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Over an Interval of Length Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c5effa2-0f66-5088-910a-cc207c28c2bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c5effa2-0f66-5088-910a-cc207c28c2bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287239} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa835eae-3e4a-5f59-a93a-d908e80541b2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287238}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4153fc64-9acf-5acd-b06a-cef20959506b" :question/math-academy-id "q-345633" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{1/2}^{1/2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3-2{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the lower and upper limits are the same. So, the integral evaluates to zero:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1/2}^{1/2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3-2{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287240 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff2f1a9d-2b0e-5a18-98d5-4f49f6f1527b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Given Another Integral With Reversed Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4f2682f-d8fe-540f-b2cc-bd5707f362bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287242} {:answer/id #uuid "e69df18e-b95f-585c-a50b-3ac706dbb4bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4f2682f-d8fe-540f-b2cc-bd5707f362bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287242} :answer-field/id #uuid "06ece43e-61c9-59cc-98ed-5ab9945b8915" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287241}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2d72efcd-4dfe-5261-8d75-86d82c839657" :question/math-academy-id "q-346051" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{6}^{1}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-7,$ calculate \n${\\int }_{1}^{6}(2f(x)-1)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{6}(2f(x)-1)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{6}^{1}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{6}(2f(x)-1)\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{1}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,-\\,{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2(7)\\,-\\,x\\,{|}_{1}^{6} \\\\  & =14\\,-\\,(6-1) \\\\  & =14\\,-\\,5 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287244 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff2f1a9d-2b0e-5a18-98d5-4f49f6f1527b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Given Another Integral With Reversed Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e15b143b-eafb-5b87-a8dc-4a16ec1aa25e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287246} {:answer/id #uuid "6cf86f9e-790d-5745-857c-1df6f91fc584" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e15b143b-eafb-5b87-a8dc-4a16ec1aa25e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287246} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad9497f7-a6bd-57c1-aaf5-1c8b8e0f4b4a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "246ea688-360e-5196-a83d-e47bea5b22da" :question/math-academy-id "q-346057" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{4}^{1}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-3,$ calculate ${\\int }_{1}^{4}(2f(x)+x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{4}(2f(x)+x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{4}^{1}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}(2f(x)+x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{1}^{4}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2(3)\\,+\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =6\\,+\\,\\frac{16-1}{2} \\\\  & =6\\,+\\,\\frac{15}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287248 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9693b76e-ba77-5314-bfbf-508b25bb1072" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Given Integrals Over Adjacent Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "937e73e5-8f69-5b1f-8089-87434d2a8fdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287250} {:answer/id #uuid "cb791b1c-a9ea-52b6-8898-f468d6f30bbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb791b1c-a9ea-52b6-8898-f468d6f30bbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287251} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d118722-2245-5229-9b6b-01a2481dbf7d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eec642e8-aa00-5555-b355-9ec662ad96f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-346188" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2$ and ${\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-5$ then what is ${\\int }_{1}^{6}(2f(x)+4)\\,\\text{d}x?$\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{6}(2f(x)+4)\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information gives\n\n$$\n2+(-5)={\\int }_{1}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,⇒\\,{\\int }_{1}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-3.\n$$\n\nFinally, compute the desired integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{6}(2f(x)+4)\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{1}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+4{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2(-3)+4\\cdot x\\,{|}_{1}^{6} \\\\  & =-6+4(6-1) \\\\  & =-6+20 \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287252 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9693b76e-ba77-5314-bfbf-508b25bb1072" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Given Integrals Over Adjacent Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dee722b9-9bc9-5851-b8be-fbdcd71df4e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dee722b9-9bc9-5851-b8be-fbdcd71df4e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287254} :answer-field/id #uuid "65179503-8e8e-558f-82ed-4dd001000cfc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "365f9a98-2f82-5cd8-98a6-e5a67719486f" :question/math-academy-id "q-346189" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=7$ and ${\\int }_{4}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$ then what is ${\\int }_{0}^{8}(3+f(x))\\,\\text{d}x?$\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{8}(3+f(x))\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{4}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7+1={\\int }_{0}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,⇒\\,{\\int }_{0}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, compute the desired integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{8}(3+f(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{0}^{8}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot x\\,{|}_{0}^{8}+8 \\\\  & =3(8-0)+8 \\\\  & =24+8 \\\\  & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287255 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d752aac-6187-595d-8818-b0179fd7380e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Using Multiple Properties of Definite Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa12a659-9565-53af-b941-8827250b633b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287257}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa12a659-9565-53af-b941-8827250b633b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287257} :answer-field/id #uuid "08ddec81-4b31-587c-8e65-9645ef73232a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287256}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "18cab19d-8f67-5d6e-9e84-4c7aa4d64f7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-346225" :question/problem "Let $f$ and $g$ be continuous functions such that ${\\int }_{0}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=9,\\,\\,{\\int }_{0}^{8}\\frac{1}{2}\\,g(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-4,$ and ${\\int }_{3}^{8}(3f(x)+g(x))\\,\\text{d}x=10.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{0}^{3}(3f(x)+g(x))\\,\\text{d}x?$\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{3}(3f(x)+g(x))\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify one of the given integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{8}\\frac{1}{2}\\,g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-4 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{8}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-4 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{8}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the adjacent intervals rule and substituting known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{8}(3f(x)+g(x))\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(3f(x)+g(x))\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{8}(3f(x)+g(x))\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the sum rule for definite integrals,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{8}(3f(x)+g(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{0}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{8}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3(9)+(-8) \\\\  & =19.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(3f(x)+g(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =19-{\\int }_{3}^{8}(3f(x)+g(x))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =19-10 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287258 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d752aac-6187-595d-8818-b0179fd7380e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Using Multiple Properties of Definite Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea337439-3768-513f-830f-a6043d4f5289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287260}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea337439-3768-513f-830f-a6043d4f5289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287260} :answer-field/id #uuid "54f606cc-6eec-50d2-90c6-fce418e55397" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e93e9065-9494-5342-89db-24beca909847" :question/math-academy-id "q-346226" :question/problem "Let $f$ and $g$ be continuous functions such that ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-4,\\,\\,{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\frac{2}{3}\\,g(x)\\,\\text{d}x=6,$ and ${\\int }_{-1}^{3}(\\frac{5}{2}f(x)-4g(x))\\,\\text{d}x=-20.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{-3}^{-1}(\\frac{5}{2}f(x)-4g(x))\\,\\text{d}x?$\n\n${\\int }_{-3}^{-1}(\\frac{5}{2}f(x)-4g(x))\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify one of the given integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\frac{2}{3}\\,g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =6 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =6 \\\\ {\\int }_{-3}^{3}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the adjacent intervals rule and substituting known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}(\\frac{5}{2}f(x)-4g(x))\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-3}^{-1}(\\frac{5}{2}f(x)-4g(x))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,+{\\int }_{-1}^{3}(\\frac{5}{2}f(x)-4g(x))\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the sum rule for definite integrals,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}(\\frac{5}{2}f(x)-4g(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x-4{\\int }_{-3}^{3}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}(-4)-4(9) \\\\  & =-46.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}(\\frac{5}{2}f(x)-4g(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =-46-{\\int }_{-1}^{3}(\\frac{5}{2}f(x)-4g(x))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-46-(-20) \\\\  & =-26.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287261 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d752aac-6187-595d-8818-b0179fd7380e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Using Multiple Properties of Definite Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b11126c-e054-57bd-9dbc-db1779838a71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287263} {:answer/id #uuid "de088edb-8704-5be7-a106-c32d7afd6c1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287264} {:answer/id #uuid "4a14cc77-4818-5ce4-a31c-4b7a5e5e26c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287265} {:answer/id #uuid "9f2b1a05-a696-5bcd-83b1-46477bb4df58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287266} {:answer/id #uuid "f9fb6bc0-3cf2-5f5c-a0b8-279c58c1c77a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287267}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b11126c-e054-57bd-9dbc-db1779838a71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287263} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff98cde1-3450-5181-83f4-c8aa84e70126" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b49ad214-b312-5d79-8063-a03e0ca82e97" :question/math-academy-id "q-4790" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a function such that ${\\int }_{3}^{0}\\frac{1}{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-1$ and ${\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=6.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify one of the given integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{0}\\frac{1}{3}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{3}^{0}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, from the adjacent intervals rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3+6 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287268 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d752aac-6187-595d-8818-b0179fd7380e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Using Multiple Properties of Definite Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f247bf84-33b6-5c85-b3b3-a4b8ce8abb7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287270} {:answer/id #uuid "27a01490-a60e-51e2-a28f-fb28b2004451" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287271} {:answer/id #uuid "c6095231-508c-5673-bd3c-3f4b3815da10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287272} {:answer/id #uuid "93c1daa6-65f9-5d3b-ad89-6e1227208de9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287273} {:answer/id #uuid "c9dd6c17-be8d-52fb-9beb-a964d86a9fa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287274}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93c1daa6-65f9-5d3b-ad89-6e1227208de9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287273} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4b5f5cc-c453-599b-b999-95893c6bc7d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287269}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "41e5e2a8-7447-512a-b492-6cb29cfa7129" :question/math-academy-id "q-4796" :question/problem "Suppose that $g(x)$ is a  function such that  ${\\int }_{4}^{8}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x=5$ and ${\\int }_{4}^{1}3g(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-6.$  What is the value of ${\\int }_{1}^{8}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify one of the given integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{1}3g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-6 \\\\ 3{\\int }_{4}^{1}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-6 \\\\ -3{\\int }_{1}^{4}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-6 \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{4}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, from the adjacent intervals rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{8}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{4}^{8}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+5 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078951 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff2f1a9d-2b0e-5a18-98d5-4f49f6f1527b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Given Another Integral With Reversed Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "840a73f4-cd00-5060-8970-125b44881219" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076918} {:answer/id #uuid "f9b6c093-2259-5ba4-89ca-aee6d959ede3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076919} {:answer/id #uuid "13dfae14-52db-5679-a8bc-82045064ac63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076920} {:answer/id #uuid "4dc15368-5973-5d55-8088-439c77a8bdc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076921} {:answer/id #uuid "7aa99ec8-79af-5880-8a3b-caf7bd4400c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076922}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13dfae14-52db-5679-a8bc-82045064ac63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076920} :answer-field/id #uuid "161475ce-a08f-5735-b906-871a8bac44fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9bed53a1-bdd6-5f20-ab7f-4c6204732818" :question/math-academy-id "q-49244" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{5}^{2}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-4$, calculate\n${\\int }_{2}^{5}(2f(x)+3)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{5}^{2}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{5}(2f(x)+3)\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+3{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2(4)+3\\cdot x\\,{|}_{2}^{5} \\\\  & =8+3(5-2) \\\\  & =8+3\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =17.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
